          "heading": "What Are Resources? Classification of Resources",
          "para": "A resource is anything available in our environment which can be used to satisfy our needs, provided three conditions are met: it must be technologically accessible, economically feasible and culturally acceptable. Only then does a natural object become a 'resource' - coal underground is geology, but usable coal is a resource. On the basis of origin, resources are biotic (derived from living things - forests, animals, fish) or abiotic (non-living - rocks, metals, minerals). On the basis of exhaustibility they are renewable (regenerated quickly - solar and wind energy, water, forests, wildlife, if used carefully) or non-renewable (fixed stock over time - fossil fuels and minerals; some like metals can be recycled, but coal and petroleum cannot). By ownership they are individual (farm land, house), community (grazing grounds, burial grounds, ponds), national (minerals, roads, railways, rivers within a country) or international (ocean resources beyond 200 km of the Exclusive Economic Zone). By status of development they are potential (exist but not yet used - solar energy in Rajasthan), developed (surveyed and in use), stock (exist but lack the technology to use - hydrogen as fuel, water in rivers as energy source) and reserves (stock whose technology exists but is kept for the future - forests held for later use, dams holding back water).",
